A||N||12||Seanh||Shift Kit = Tire Chirp?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||09-15-2001||01:28 PM||||Will the shift kit that the Fiero Store sells chirp the tires into 2nd on an otherwise stock TH125c tranny? If no, are there any kits out there that will shift even harder? I want one that slams the sucker into every gear, don't give a crap about ride comfort. Where is the best place to get a real hard shifting kit for our tranny? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||Sleeper||09-15-2001||01:55 PM||||i bought a shift kit from IRM. it shifts hard but not enough to chrip the tires. try building a tranny if you want it to chrip.||||reg||1
Z||000002||voyagerspe||09-15-2001||02:27 PM||||ive done the shift kit in my 3.4 87 gt<BR>chirps good in second.<BR>in my 85 gt (2.8) i get a small chirp <P>car is still smooth to drive only shifts hard with the hammer down.||||reg||14
Z||000003||Seanh||09-15-2001||02:51 PM||||Are you useing the one from The Fiero Store voyagerspe? If no, which one are you useing? That sounds like the one I want. It will be used with a turbocharged 3.4L engine.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ronnie||09-15-2001||10:42 PM||||What is involved in installing a shift kit? Is it something I might can do myself, or need a tranny shop to install?||||reg||6
Z||000005||Seanh||09-16-2001||01:15 PM||||I think it depends on what kit you use, but the Fiero Store one I'v heard only involves pulling the pan off and changing some components like springs and stuff. I'v never installed a shift kit in a tranny while in the car, only one I ever did was a "Transpak" into a TH350 tranny, that was a SOB even with the tranny out of the car. It is a really setup though, it elimates all automatic operation of the auto tranny.||||reg||1
Z||000006||85ZainoGT||09-16-2001||02:05 PM||||i got a completely stock 2.8 v6 85gt, and chirp them into second..||||reg||1
Z||000007||voyagerspe||09-16-2001||02:17 PM||||i bought the kit from the fiero store <P>its level of difficulty is about intermediate. <BR>if your comfortable pulling an engine apart you should have no problem with this.  If your uncomfortable with changing your sparkplugs HIRE SOMEONE to do this.  ||||reg||14
Z||000008||Ronnie||09-16-2001||02:53 PM||||Thanx for the info. I just have never even looked at a kit to see what all comes with it. I didn't know if it was just a pull-the-pan type of job or not.||||reg||6
Z||000009||theogre||09-16-2001||06:13 PM||||Keep in mind that beating on a TH125 or it's 4 speed relative are good ways to break them. These transmissions will only take so much abuse even with a shift kit. they simply aren't build for repeated poundings.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Seanh||09-16-2001||11:25 PM||||Is the 125c really that bad? I though it was actually a little more durable then most other trannys out there. Is the TH125c hard to find? Id like to build my own tranny, but want to find a junk yard tranny to use then swap into my car. What is the 4spd auto tranny used? Iv heard of a could differnt ones, but which is the easiest. I have a 4spd in mine, but I wasn't the one to install it, nor am I 100% sure which one it even is. I'd like to find either a 125c or its 4spd version to build, so if anyone has one or knows of a local junk yard that does, please let me know.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||09-16-2001||11:27 PM||||BTW, where is a good website that sells high performance/heavy duty tranny parts for either the 125c or the 4spd auto?||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||09-16-2001||11:46 PM||||There is little or no aftermarket for "performance" parts for the TH124 and 440-T40(4T60) four speed.<P>If you are going to do this, find a "new" a unit as posible. The later models have a number of internal improvments. The early units have known weaknesses.<P>If you are going to do these kind of things I recomend a full rebuild and a new chain in addition to the shiftkit.<P>If you are going to rebuild on I suggest <A HREF="http://www.txchange.com/" TARGET=_blank>Transmission Exchange Co.</A> I haven't bought from them myself yet but the couple times I talked to them about some stuff that had every bit of info I needed. They have complete parts kits and manuals. You should also subscribe to ALLDATA DIY or find some other source of GM TSB's there are at least a dozen on the TH125 alone. The TSB's cover weaknesses and design changes not listed in many other sources.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
